.TH SMTP 2
.SH NAME
smtp \- Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "smtp.m";
smtp := load Smtp Smtp->PATH;

open:     fn(server: string): (int, string);
authopen: fn(user, password, server: string, mode: int): (int, string);
sendmail: fn(fromwhom: string, 
             towhom: list of string, 
             cc: list of string,
             msg: list of string): (int, string);
close:    fn(): (int, string);
okaddr:   fn(addr: string): int;
okheader: fn(value: string): int;
timeout:  fn(ms: int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Smtp
provides an interface to the mail transport protocol SMTP.
.PP
.B Open
opens a connection to the given SMTP
.IR server .
If
.I server
is nil,
.B open
uses the
default mail server
.BR $smtp ,
which must be defined in
.IR ndb (6).
It returns -1 and an error message if the connection fails.
.PP
.B Authopen
opens a connection over TLS and authenticates as
.I user
with
.I password
(AUTH PLAIN).
.I Mode
is
.B IMPLICIT_TLS
for TLS from the start, on port 465, or
.B STARTTLS
for a connection in clear on port 587 upgraded with STARTTLS
(RFC 3207); a server that does not offer STARTTLS, or refuses it, is
refused, and nothing is sent in clear but EHLO and STARTTLS.
.BR PLAIN ,
which would send the password in clear, is refused.
.I Server
is a host, or
.IB host ! port
for another port; the certificate is checked against the host.
.PP
.B Sendmail
sends mail to the SMTP server for subsequent delivery.
The first argument names the sender, the list
.I towhom
names the
recipients,
.I cc
is a list of CC's,
and
.I msg
has the text of the message. The latter
may simply be a list of one item containing the whole message, a list of lines of the message
or any intermediate format (an empty item is an empty line). It returns -1 and an error message on failure.
The sender and each recipient must be an address as
.B okaddr
has it, or nothing is sent: an address goes on a command's line, and
one with a line break in it would be further commands.
A message with a NUL in it, or with a carriage return inside a header
line, is refused; a carriage return inside a line of the body ends
that line, so that no receiver reads past it a dot that the sender
did not double.
The message is sent only when the server answers
.B DATA
with 354, and every reply is judged by its code.
.PP
.B Okaddr
says whether
.I addr
can stand between < and > in
.B "MAIL FROM"
and
.BR "RCPT TO" :
it is not empty and has no blank, control character, DEL, < or >.
.B Okheader
says whether
.I value
stays one header's value: it has no CR, LF or NUL.
Callers that make a message's headers from text they were given
(another message's subject or id) should ask.
.PP
.B Timeout
sets how long, in milliseconds, the connection's making and each reply
are waited for (30 seconds by default).
After a timeout, a failed write or a reply that is not SMTP's, the
connection is closed and its processes are gone.
.PP
No error says what was sent as a credential: a refused or unanswered
.B AUTH
is reported as
.BR "AUTH credentials" ,
with the server's reply, less the credential if the server says it
back.
.PP
.B Close
closes the connection to the SMTP server.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/smtp.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/mailconn.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sendmail (1),
.IR acme (1),
.IR mailconn (2)
